{
  "id": "murphy-shakti8-feelgood-1phase-temporofrontal",
  "name": "8 Coil Shakti Feelgood: one phase, temporo-frontal (user variant)",
  "category": "mood",
  "evidence": "anecdote",
  "origin": "An 8 Coil Shakti user, reported on Todd Murphy's blog; variant of Murphy's one-phase Feelgood session",
  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "A user-reported variant of Murphy's one-phase Feelgood session with the four coils moved from the frontal lobes to the temporo-frontal area, where the frontal and temporal lobes meet. Same signal: the amygdala and hippocampal signals one after the other with 4-s pauses.",
  "notes": "The report names the area but not the sites; F7, FT7, F8 and FT8 (on CH5-8; CH1-4 stay silent) are an inference, and all four carry the same signal at once. Polarity is an inference too: each side gets one + and one - coil, as every Shakti channel has ('You need TWO poles (positive and negative) to make ONE magnetic field'), in the pattern of the vendor's frontal photo (upper left and lower right reversed: F7 and FT8, weight -1). The user reported 30-min and 60-min sessions: phase 1 (30 min) is the 30-min version; stop after it, or let phase 2 run for the 60-min version. The vendor's own Feelgood phases are 20 or 30 min. This placement is one user's report, not a vendor design. The vendor's signals are proprietary. One pass here is: the amygdala signal, approximated by the built-in accelerating burst (6 to 30 Hz, 230 points of 3 ms = 0.69 s; the 230-point length is from Ryczko & Persinger 2002); 4 s of silence (the vendor: 'generated one after the other ... with a four-second latency'); the hippocampal 'LTP' signal, approximated by four theta bursts of 4 pulses at 100 Hz (the Larson & Lynch timing of the project's ltp generator) at the 5.4 Hz spectral peak the vendor reports for it (0.6 s, with 1-ms points instead of the vendor's 3-ms points so the 100 Hz pulses fit); then 4 s of silence again. That gives about 8 s of field per minute (the vendor states 9.6 s). The vendor says you may feel 'a bit foggy' right after, and that too many frontal (or temporal) series can produce 'malaise', so alternate frontal and temporal series. Intensity: the Shakti software defaults to 50% volume, which the vendor calls the 50 mG 'laboratory standard' (5 µT), while its measurement page gives 10 mG (1 µT) as the normal peak; no independent measurement of a Shakti unit was found, and this file uses 1 µT. Persinger's lab reported better mood with weekly burst-firing fields in small samples (Baker-Price & Persinger 1996, 2003, the latter with temporal or left-frontal placement; Tsang et al. 2009); none has been independently replicated. An independent randomized sham-controlled test of the 8 Coil Shakti amygdala signal over the temporal lobes found no effect on emotional responses (Gendle & McGrath 2012; Murphy argues it used the wrong session), and a double-blind God Helmet test found no field effect (Granqvist 2005). The same lab reported more aggression after three weekly bilateral burst-firing sessions (Gillis & Persinger 1993) and more acceptance of false statements with burst-firing over the left prefrontal region (Ross, Koren & Persinger 2008). Not a treatment for depression or any illness: the vendor excludes people with psychiatric diagnoses. Not for people with epilepsy; stop if distressed. Revised after a re-check of the vendor's Feelgood pages (2026): coil polarity added (all four coils had the same polarity before), and the 'not in sensory deprivation' rule and the vendor's intensity figures noted. Still approximate: the sites, the polarity and both signal templates.",
  "setting": "Not in sensory deprivation: the vendor says the Feelgood session 'should not be done in sensory deprivation', since it is meant for 'outward-looking', 'this-worldly' effects. Evenings, so that any effect is noticed the next morning, as the vendor advises.",
  "schedule": "Vendor: 6 weekly sessions or 6 sessions every 3 days (never more often than every 72 h), then 3 weeks off. If a series is interrupted, wait 3 days and restart.",
  "targets": ["left-temporal-pole", "right-temporal-pole", "lateral-orbitofrontal-cortex"],
